{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to see the faint light from objects\nin deepest space, astronomers go to the darkest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5460,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "places on the planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7140,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to listen to their quite radio signals,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "astronomers head as far away as \npossible from radio-noisy humans."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16080,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there is nowhere on the earth, or even \norbiting the Earth, that’s far enough to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21420,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hear to the faint radio hum from the time\nbefore stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25680,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, we may need to build a giant radio\ntelescope in the quietest place in the solar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31080,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system—the far side of the moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38420,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Putting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39420,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a giant telescope on the Moon sounds\nlike a cool idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42480,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, the moon has essentially no atmosphere\nso the sky is crystal clear."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46860,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that nice stable surface means would could\npotentially build a large telescope right"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52680,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there—perhaps a much larger one than we\ncould put in orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56700,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "People have been talking about doing this\nfor decades, but the closest we’ve gotten"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61020,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the ultraviolet scope on China’s Chang’e\n3 lander - with its 2-inch diameter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68460,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So is the idea of a proper giant telescope\njust a sci-fi fantasy?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74400,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76140,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meet the Lunar Crater Radio Telescope—a\ngiant radio antenna filling a crater on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82080,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "far side of the moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83400,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If it’s built, it will be one of the largest\ntelescopes ever built, and by far the largest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88320,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outside our atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89700,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it’ll see further than any existing\ntelescope, peering into the time before the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94560,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first stars and galaxies ever formed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96060,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it may indeed get built."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98280,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2020 the Lunar Crater Radio Telescope received\na half million dollars in funding from the NASA"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105120,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Innovative Advanced Concepts program to explore\nits feasibility."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109140,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s going to take quite a bit more work\nto convince NASA to green-light the project,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but first let’s see if we can even convince\nourselves that this is a good idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118680,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First let’s remind ourselves real quick\nwhy it’s nice to have telescopes in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122880,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s because of Earth’s pesky atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125760,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For most wavelengths of light, that thick\nblanket of breathable, transparent gas is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130020,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually a thick blanket of impenetrable fog."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132360,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gamma rays, X-rays, and most ultraviolet is\nstopped in the upper atmosphere, while only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138180,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "narrow bands of infrared light get past the\nwater and CO2 molecules to reach the surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143940,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This leaves only two windows on earth’s\nsurface where nearly 100% of photons make"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148560,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it to the ground—visible light and a section\nof the radio spectrum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152220,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To see most of the EM spectrum we need to\nput telescopes in space—JWST for infrared,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157980,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Chandra for X-rays, Hubble for ultraviolet,\netc."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160920,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And Hubble is even great for visible light\nfor a different reason—it avoids the blur"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165240,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "introduced by the atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167280,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for a long time, radio astronomy has been\nleft out of the space telescope game."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171960,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not only is the atmosphere transparent to\na huge window of radio light, it also doesn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177180,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "introduce the same blur."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178620,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For that reason, we’ve been pretty happy\nbuilding our giant radio telescopes on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182880,
    "dDurationMs": 600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface of the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183480,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why put one in space?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185820,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, radio photons with wavelengths from\naround 1cm to 10m can pass through the atmosphere"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193020,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just fine."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193740,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But wavelengths longer than that tend to get\nreflected by the ionosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199260,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is where the last wisps of the atmosphere\nare fading into the near vacuum of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204720,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The intense solar radiation up there separates\nelectrons from their atoms, wreathing the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210300,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planet in a shell of charged particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212700,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When a radio photon reaches the ionosphere\nit will jiggle an electron, and that jiggling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217680,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron will send the photon right back where\nit came from."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220020,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So our planet is surrounded by a \nsort of double sided radio mirror."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223620,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This can actually be handy—we can send radio \nsignals extremely long distances across the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228660,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface of the Earth by bouncing \nthem off the ionosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232260,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it also means that from the surface, space\nis completely dark to long-wavelength radio"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238200,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light. In fact even low-earth orbit \nsatellites are effected by the ionosphere"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242940,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and by the extremely radio \nnoisy local primate population."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247980,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which menas we have literally never looked at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251100,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe across an enormous \nrange of the electromagnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253980,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spectrum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255180,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s what the lunar crater radio telescope\nwill allow us to do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258900,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now while I fully support peering at the universe\nin new ways just to see what we’ll see,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264240,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s probably not enough to convince NASA."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267000,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re going to want to be sure that we\nlearn something important if we expand our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271320,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vision that way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272700,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, there’s always the fact that this\nhuge blind spot in the EM spectrum corresponds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278100,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to an entire cosmic epoch that we’ve never\nglimpsed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282780,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, the oldest light we can detect is the\ncosmic microwave background radiation, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287460,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was emitted when the universe \nwas around 370,000 years old."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291960,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The hot plasma of hydrogen and helium that\nfilled the early universe back then cooled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296820,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the point that electrons and nuclei could\nform the first atoms, rendering the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301320,
    "dDurationMs": 900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transparent to light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302820,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The photons released at that moment were mostly\ninfrared, but since then they’ve traveled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307980,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for 13 and a half billion years through a\nuniverse that expanded by a factor of over"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312420,
    "dDurationMs": 540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1000."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312960,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We say that the light has been redshifted—from\ninfrared to the millimeter wavelength photons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319080,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the cosmic microwave background."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320940,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But after the release of the CMB there’s\na huge gap in the fossil record."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327660,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At some point, all that hydrogen and helium\ngas collapsed to form the first stars and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332160,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxies, lighting up the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334140,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The James Webb Space Telescope is seeing some\nof this very early starlight."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339960,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But between the release of the CMB and the\ntime when star formation really took off there’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344940,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a gap of at least a 100 million years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347100,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call these the cosmic dark ages, and it's \ncrucial for understanding everything that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352440,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happened after."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353460,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was during this time that gravity began\nto pull matter together into the massive halos"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358500,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that would form superclusters of galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360600,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the moment, everything we know comes from\nour simulations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365520,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But with a giant moon telescope, we could\nactually peer into this time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369540,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And see … what?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371760,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If there were no stars or galaxies\nwhat light would even look for?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376860,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The matter that would go on to form stars\nwould be in the form of hydrogen and helium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382560,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gas that would quickly be losing the heat\nit had when it first formed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386820,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As such, it would emit very little light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389100,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But not NO light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391920,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the electron in atomic hydrogen flips\nthe direction of its spin, it emits a very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397620,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "low energy photon of radio light with a wavelength\nof 21cm."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402840,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you point a radio antenna at the space\nbetween the stars in the Milky Way and tune"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407820,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the receiver through the wavelengths, you’ll\nget a spike of signal at 21cm."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411540,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we’ve actually mapped the cold gas in\nthe Milky Way by looking at this light with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417300,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our big Earth-based radio telescopes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419220,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now any 21 cm radiation emitted during the \ncosmic dark ages will have been redshifted by the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424860,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425820,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, a 21 cm photon emitted when the \nuniverse was just say 17 million years old will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433020,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be stretched to 21 meters by the time it reaches\nus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437040,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More distant, earlier gas will be seen at\nlonger wavelengths still."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441480,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While regular 21 cm radio light \nreaches the Earth’s surface,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445680,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the stuff coming to us from the dark ages"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448260,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has been redshifted to the point that it bounces\nright off our ionosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So perhaps you’re starting to see why a \nspace radio telescope might be a good idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457680,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, sure, but why a giant one, and why on\nthe moon?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462000,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the bigger the telescope the more sensitive\nit is, so that’s nice."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465540,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s a more critical reason for giant-ness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468360,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to detect light of a certain wavelength,\nyou need a telescope that’s at least as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473340,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big as that wavelength."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475200,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And to cleanly bounce that light around in \norder to make an image, the telescope should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480660,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be quite a bit larger than that wavelength."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482820,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact the resolution of a telescope depends "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484980,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the ratio of its aperture \nsize to the wavelength"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487500,
    "dDurationMs": 780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s looking at."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488280,
    "dDurationMs": 6231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to get a good resolution looking \nat 10s of meter-long radio photons, you’re"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494511,
    "dDurationMs": 3429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to need a telescope that hundred \nof meters in diameter at least."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498780,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that does sound like a big scope."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500880,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was an enormous challenge to get JWST’s\n6 and a half meter mirror safely into space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507360,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But mirrors for infrared, visible, and ultraviolet\nlight are heavy, fragile glass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513000,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Radio telescope “mirrors” can be a simple\nmesh of conducting material."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517740,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember I said that the ionosphere reflects\nradio waves because it contains electrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522900,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for those waves to move around."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525720,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, metal wire has moveable electrons, and\nso a radio wave hitting a wire mesh will just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532800,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bounce off as long as the wire grid size is\nsufficiently smaller than the wavelength of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538140,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interest."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539160,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That reflecting surface still has to be the\nright shape—and that shape is a paraboloid—a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545160,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "circular parabola—just as with regular telescope\nmirrors."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548400,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A paraboloid will focus incoming waves coming\nfrom a particular direction to a single point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555120,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most radio telescopes swivel to point at the\nobject of interest."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558360,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But above a certain size, swiveling becomes\nimpracticable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561300,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing, the shape of the dish warps\nas it moves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564600,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s why our largest radio dishes were\nbuilt fixed in place in valleys, like China’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569940,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "500m diameter FAST telescope or Puerto Rico’s\nslightly smaller Arecibo … before it collapsed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576420,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rest in peace buddy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577860,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And just a side note—because it’s cool \nhow these fixed dish telescopes work."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582420,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They have a feed antenna—which \nis the detector—that "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585420,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can slide around to the focal points of light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587460,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coming in from different directions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588840,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Arecibo used an elongated feed antenna to\ncorrect for its spherical dish shape, while"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594540,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "FAST uses movable panels to warp the dish\ninto a paraboloid pointing at the feed antenna"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600960,
    "dDurationMs": 120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "location."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601080,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Lunar Crater Radio Telescope will be a\n350 meter diameter fixed-dish—so at bit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607140,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bigger than Arecibo and a bit smaller than\nFAST."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609420,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now this isn’t a new idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611760,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There have been proposals to build fixed-dish\nradio telescopes on the moon since the 60s."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616800,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the reasons these never went anywhere\nis that it’s not really possible to build"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620580,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an Arecibo equivalent there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622500,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Both Arecibo and FAST heavy support structures\nto hold them in the correct shape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627660,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This requirement is one of the things that\nkilled previous proposals."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631740,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was, and still is, too difficult to transport\nthe heavy support structure required to maintain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637740,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the shape of a moon telescope."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639660,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the Lunar Crater Radio Telescope has a\nbeautiful workaround—just hang the telescope"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645300,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like a giant space hammock."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Normally when you hang a thread between two\npoints you get a shape called a catenary,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651960,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not a parabola."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654180,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that’s only if the rope has a constant\nthickness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656940,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the wires are thicker at the edges and\nbecome thinner towards the center in just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661020,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the right way, that thread will hang as a\nparabola."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663780,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it’s possible to create a perfect parabolic\ndish with no supports besides whatever tethers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669300,
    "dDurationMs": 540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it to the edge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669840,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This design will also safely withstand the\n300 Kelvin range of temperatures it will be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675000,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exposed to—a fluctuation that could damage\na more rigid architecture, although actual"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observations will only happen during lunar\nnight when the radio-noisy sun is out of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686160,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way and the temperature fluctuation is more\nlike 10 Kelvin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688500,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The new LCRT design calls for the entire reflector\nmesh to weigh about half a ton, with a total"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694800,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "launch mass around 3 times that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696540,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is well within the capabilities \nof current launch technology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s one thing to get a giant net to\nthe moon and entirely another to set it up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let me talk you through the current plan,\nwith this nice animation produced by the LCRT"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712080,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "team."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713160,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The spacecraft containing the folded-up telescope\nlands in the center of the chosen crater—and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718980,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ll come back to how that’s selected."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720480,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then harpoons are fired beyond the crater\nrim."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723840,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are pulled tight and then the feed antenna\nis raised to what will be the focal point"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728340,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the dish."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728820,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then the mesh is unfolded by pulling it up\ntowards the rim."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By adjusting the tethers, the shape of the\nreflector can be adjusted to a nice, round"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737760,
    "dDurationMs": 840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paraboloid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738600,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so we know how we might go about installing\na lunar crater radio telescope."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743340,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We just need to choose a crater."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746160,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We want it to be on the far side, and preferably\ndeep into the far side to minimize interference"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751560,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the moon’s annoyingly radio-loud planetary\nneighbour."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755100,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since the telescope won’t be steerable,\nit’ll point wherever that spot on the moon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759720,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is pointing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761280,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means it sweeps out a ring as the moon\nrotates once per month."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765120,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That ring is as large as possible if the telescope\nis on the lunar equator."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769320,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unfortunately, that exact location sweeps\nthrough the Milky Way, which is loud with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774240,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radio signals that we don’t care about."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Positioned around 15 degrees north of the\nequator gives us a large circle above the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782280,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galactic disk, which gives us a much clearer\nview of the beginning of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787020,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Add the restriction of a \nnice, round, boulder-clear, "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789480,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and relatively deep crater really cuts down"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791640,
    "dDurationMs": 540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the options."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792180,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The current top candidate is this little guy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795240,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At 1.3km across and nearly 300 meters deep,\nit’s small enough that it doesn’t even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801600,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have a name."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802800,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However it’s large enough to comfortably\nfit our proposed new toy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806460,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As nice as it’ll be to have the entire mass\nof the moon acting as a shield against human"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811020,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radio interference, it does make communication\nwith the observatory challenging."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816300,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Radio telescopes in particular collect an\nenormous amount of data."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819840,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how do we transmit it back to Earth?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822660,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll probably need a dedicated relay satellite,\nperhaps orbiting the Moon so it uploads new"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827280,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "data on the far side and transmits it on the\nnear side."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830040,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or perhaps a relay parked at the Earh-Moon\nL2 Lagrange point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834420,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, is NASA going to fully fund this?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837720,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The proposal certainly seems plausible, and\nthe science is very compelling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However there are other options for looking into \nthe cosmic dark ages besides specifically a radio"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849360,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "telescope in a lunar crater."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, there are plans to cover much\nlarger regions of the moon’s far side in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simple dipole antennae, or even build such\na dipole array from a swarm of satellites"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862440,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in orbit around the moon or sit at the L2 point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866820,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are advantages and disadvantages to "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869700,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all these approaches—and possibly \na combination is the real answer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873840,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Honestly, turning the far side of the moon\ninto a giant radio telescope with crater dishes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879300,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and dipole arrays sounds too cool not to do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882840,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But I’m not NASA, so we’ll have to wait\nand see what they decide is the smartest way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887940,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to peer into the time before stars at\nthe beginning of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894060,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. Before we get to comments we \nwanted to let you know about our Patreon.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898200,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s an incredibly helpful way to support this \nshow and ensure our survival long-term. If you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903660,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "join there’s lots of amazing perks \nincluding monthly Q\u0026A livestreams,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907260,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "access to episode scripts, and even a private \nchat with me depending on level. And every  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912720,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "level includes access to our discord where you \ncan interact with other community members 24/7.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918480,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another way to support the show is by visiting \nour merch store where you can score some sweet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923640,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space Time swag. And as always, subscribing, \nhitting the bell notification and joining the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928980,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "early gang when new episodes drop is \na great way to support the community."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932580,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, Today we’re doing comment responses for \nthe last two episodes—the one about so-called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937320,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark stars—stars powered by dark matter, and \nthe one about spacetime foam—the tumultuous  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942720,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "finest scale where the geometry of space is \nconstantly changing. Starting with dark stars:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949722,
    "dDurationMs": 6858,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dmaster254 asks if dark matter can annihilate \ndoes that mean over a stupidly long timescale  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956580,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the amount of dark matter would diminish. The \nanswer is yes, but then the new question is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962400,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what effect would this have? For example, \nif dark matter in galaxies diminished,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967020,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would galaxies eventually fly apart due \nto the diminishing gravitational field?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972780,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer to the latter is that if this could \nhappen it would be in the unthinkably far future  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979380,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because the rate of interaction between dark \nmatter particles is insanely low. But, over time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984720,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yes, it might cause galaxies to fall apart. That \ntimescale depends on what dark matter actually is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990660,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but my guess is that all stars will have burned \nout before we get close to that happening."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996060,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A few of you commented to praise John Mitchel, \nthe guy who first conceived of black holes back  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in 1783—which he called dark stars, and have \nno relation to the dark-matter-powered dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008120,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars of that episode. But we did show what was \nsupposed to be a ye-olde picture of a black hole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014420,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and some of you wondered whether this was \nMitchel’s drawing. The answer is no—we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020480,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just made that graphic for fun. It’s based on \nthe black hole from the movie Interstellar,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024620,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which the light from the accretion disk \naround the black hole is warped due to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intense gravitational field. The interstellar \nblack hole was based on real simulations that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033680,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "used Einstein’s general relativity to calculate \nthe deflection of light in warped spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038360,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "John Mitchel came up with the idea of dark stars \n132 years before general relativity, so had no  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1045200,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "idea about warped spacetime. His black hole, \nwhich he called dark stars, were imagined as stars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1051080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so massive that their light was slowed to the \npoint of stopping and falling back on the star.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056900,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know that this doesn’t happen—the speed of \nlight isn’t changed due to a gravitational field.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061940,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Its energy is still sapped, but that manifests as \nthe wavelengths stretching—redshifting—and in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1068240,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "case of black holes a photon trying to escape from \nthe event horizon is redshifted to non-existence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1072380,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it turns out that the slowing of light gives \nthe same answer for the size of an event horizon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as the redshirfing of light. And if light slows in \ngravitational fields then it should indeed also be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083240,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deflected by them, in the same way that light is \ndeflected as it enters a medium light water where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1089240,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its speed changes. So in principle Mitchel could \nhave guessed that dark stars might look like the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095300,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interstellar black hole if he added an accretion \ndisk. However there’s no evidence that he did so."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1100700,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, moving on to spacetime foam."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102920,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kellensanna asks: if the quantum vacuum is \nfluctuating, is this something that could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1108320,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be used to perform work? Sadly not. Although \na fluctuation could in principle push, say,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1114920,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a tiny piston in a particular direction to \ndrive a tiny engine, the random nature of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1121280,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fluctuations means you can’t know which way \nthat push will happen. And even if it happens to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1127280,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be in the right direction, there’ll soon be \nanother that pushes in the wrong direction.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1132440,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the same issue with using the random motion \nof particles of air to extract energy. If you want  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1138020,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the whole story, check our “reversing \nentropy with Maxwell’s demon” episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1143600,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "GLmartins90 has a question regarding the statement \nthat photons in our Heisenberg microscope  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1148160,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "warp the fabric of spacetime—noting that we \ntypically think of only matter as doing that.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1155300,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s true that an individual photon moving \nin a single direction doesn’t warp spacetime  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1159740,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the same way that a particle with mass \ndoes. However if you trap massless photons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1164540,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a massless mirrored box then the system has \nan effective mass which does warp spacetime.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1169760,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the Heisenberg microscope thought \nexperiment, the photon has to bounce  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1173300,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off the object to be measured and back to \nthe observer. Just like the mirrored box,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1177680,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s a warping of spacetime between the \nobserver and the object, although its not quite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1181760,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same type of warping as you get with mass or \nwith a box of photons moving in all directions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1188540,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Flavorfulnuggets1 has a story to tell.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1191780,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I was walking to class one day when I was in \ncollege and Matt was walking out of my school  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1197000,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the opposite direction. I wanted to ask him so \nmany questions but the second I saw him he took  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1202460,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the hugest bite of a pastry so I just gave him \nthe double finger guns and said “HEY I KNOW YOU!”  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1207500,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thankfully he gave me the double fingers guns \nback or I would have killed myself that day"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1212120,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, flavorfulnuggest1, thanks for the whatsup. \nI remember exactly where this happened, and so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1217280,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I know it might happen again. Next time say \nhigh no matter what I’m stuffing into my face."
    } ]
  } ]
}
